Analyze this data and submit a structured recommendation using the submit_training_recommendation tool. Base all quantitative judgments strictly on the numbers provided above — do not invent or assume any data not present here. Use the user's age, sex, and BMI, if provided in userContext, to calibrate what counts as reasonable training volume and intensity for their profile. Use strengthIndex as your primary evidence for progressive overload. Use volumeSplit and routineAdherence together to judge balance and consistency. Factor in the user's long-term goal and this period's note (if provided) when shaping your advice and action items. If totalSets is 0, note that no training was logged this period rather than speculating why.

Respond in English. Every text field you submit must be plain prose only — no XML tags, no markdown formatting, no stray closing tags of any kind. The headline must stand completely on its own — write it as if it's the only sentence the user will ever read.

{
  "headline": "Every single muscle group got stronger this week — keep this momentum going.",
  "summary": "This was an excellent week of training. All six muscle groups showed meaningful strength index gains, with legs leading the charge at a jump from 108 to 115, followed closely by chest (106 to 112) and back (104 to 110). Total tonnage of 10,732 kg across 28 sets is a solid workload for a 28-year-old male at 72 kg, and the volume distribution is reasonably well-rounded across the major muscle groups. The last period's note to maintain progression and watch recovery is being honored — the numbers show you are progressing without any signs of a breakdown in performance. This is exactly the kind of week that builds long-term muscle and strength.",
  "progressiveOverload": {
    "status": "on_track",
    "notes": "Every muscle group improved its strength index this period. Legs showed the largest gain, rising from 108 to 115 (plus 7 points). Chest climbed from 106 to 112 (plus 6), back from 104 to 110 (plus 6), triceps from 104 to 109 (plus 5), shoulders from 104 to 108 (plus 4), and biceps from 102 to 106 (plus 4). These are meaningful, across-the-board improvements that confirm progressive overload is working as intended. No lift is stalling or regressing. Maintain the current progression strategy."
  },
